-## How It Works
-
-### Plan Mode (Read-Only)
-- Built-in edit/write tools disabled
-- Other active tools remain available
-- Bash commands filtered through allowlist
-- Agent creates a plan without making changes
-
-### Execution Mode
-- Full tool access restored
-- Agent executes steps in order
-- `[DONE:n]` markers track completion
-- Widget shows progress
-
-### Command Allowlist
-
-Safe commands (allowed):
-- File inspection: `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `less`, `more`
-- Search: `grep`, `find`, `rg`, `fd`
-- Directory: `ls`, `pwd`, `tree`
-- Git read: `git status`, `git log`, `git diff`, `git branch`
-- Package info: `npm list`, `npm outdated`, `yarn info`
-- System info: `uname`, `whoami`, `date`, `uptime`
-
-Blocked commands:
-- File modification: `rm`, `mv`, `cp`, `mkdir`, `touch`
-- Git write: `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`
-- Package install: `npm install`, `yarn add`, `pip install`
-- System: `sudo`, `kill`, `reboot`
-- Editors: `vim`, `nano`, `code`
+The `plan-control-result` custom session entry records `{ operation, ok,
+errorCode, state }`. State contains `sessionId`, `mode`, `planId`, `revision`,
+`steps`, and `approvedRevision`. A successful `plan_submit` returns this same state
+as JSON text and as its tool result details.
+
+Only interactive and RPC inputs can enter or approve through the controls.
+Extension-origin input cannot approve. Controls require idle execution with no
+pending messages, except that status is always read-only. Failures distinguish
+`invalid_input`, `forbidden_source`, `busy`, `no_plan`, `plan_mismatch`, and
+`stale_revision`. Repeating the same approved revision succeeds without another
+execution request. Status remains `approved` after that request settles.
+
+## Tool policy
+
+Planning exposes only the previously active subset of `read`, `grep`, `find`, and
+`ls`, plus `plan_submit`. Bash, editing, writing, and custom tools are blocked even
+if the model attempts a disabled call. Approval restores the original selection,
+including custom tools. This is a tool dispatcher policy, not an external-process
+sandbox or a restriction on malicious extensions sharing the host process.
+
+## Persistence design
+
+The extension appends `plan-mode-journal` custom entries for session initialization,
+planning entry, submission, approval, and step completion. A pure reducer reconstructs
+state in order. The entry event retains the original tool selection; submission
+events carry exact revisions; approval records the reviewed snapshot. Progress
+is separate from draft content and approval.
+
+Normal idle resume in the same persisted, file-backed session replays the journal:
+a planning cycle remains restricted, and an approved cycle restores its original
+tools without dispatching execution again. Events with a foreign session ID are
+ignored. Persisted state takes precedence over `--plan`, including normal state.
+Execution is an effect of a newly accepted approval only, never an effect of replay.
+Crash recovery, in-flight shutdown, reload, fork, and tree navigation are outside
+this example's supported resume contract.
+
+## Tests
+
+The extension tests cover the public controls, exact revisions, input origins,
+busy transitions, tool restrictions, asynchronous review, progress, and event
+replay. The existing utility functions and their tests remain unchanged.
+
+```sh
+cd packages/coding-agent
+npx vitest run test/plan-mode-extension.test.ts test/plan-mode-utils.test.ts
+```
